```-template
<template>
  <div class="campaign-materials">
    <h2>Our Party's Features</h2>
    <ul>
      <li v-for="(feature, index) in features" :key="index">
        <label :for="index">{{ feature.name }}</label>
        <input type="checkbox" :id="index" v-model="features[index].checked">
        <p>{{ feature.description }}</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      features: [
        { name: 'Feature 1', description: 'Description of Feature 1' },
        { name: 'Feature 2', description: 'Description of Feature 2' },
        // Add more features as needed
      ],
    };
  }
};
</script>
```

In this example, we're using the `v-for` directive to loop through an array of objects representing each feature offered by our party. Each object has two properties: `name`, which is the name of the feature, and `description`, which is a brief description of the feature.
We then use the `v-model` directive to bind the `checked` property of each checkbox to the corresponding feature object in the `features` array. This allows us to control which features are selected or deselected when the user clicks on a checkbox.
Finally, we can customize the appearance of the campaign materials by adding additional HTML elements and styles to the template. For example, we might add a background color or font style to make the materials look more professional or friendly.
